# Pages

`howdy/ui/page` turns a list of elements into a whole HTML document and
sends it as the response. It writes the parts every page needs, such as the
doctype, the character set and the viewport tag, and it makes sure the
theme and the styles for your components are in place before the browser
paints anything.

## A page

```gleam
import howdy/controller.{type Context}
import howdy/ui
import howdy/ui/page
import lustre/element.{text}

fn orders(ctx: Context) {
  page.new("Orders")
  |> page.body([
    ui.container([], [
      ui.h1("Orders"),
      ui.p([text("Nothing here yet.")]),
    ]),
  ])
  |> page.respond(ctx)
}
```

`page.new` takes the title and starts a page with sensible defaults:
English as the language, and the built-in light and dark themes. The
functions after it each adjust one thing, and `page.respond` finishes the
page and answers the request with `text/html`.

The elements in the body are [Lustre](https://hexdocs.pm/lustre) elements.
The components in `howdy/ui` are one source of them, and anything from
`lustre/element/html` works beside them. Lustre escapes text and attribute
values for you, so it is safe to put user input into a page.

## Building the page

- `page.body(elements)` adds elements to the `<body>`. Call it more than
  once and the elements are added in order, which is handy when a shared
  function adds the header and the handler adds the rest.
- `page.head(elements)` adds elements to the `<head>`, after the title and
  the styles. Use it for a description, icons, fonts or your own scripts.
- `page.lang(code)` sets the `lang` attribute, which screen readers and
  search engines use. The default is `"en"`.
- `page.theme(name)` selects a theme for this response.
- `page.themes(themes)` replaces the themes the page offers.
- `page.stylesheet(at: path)` links a CSS file, where the page would
  otherwise embed its styles.
- `page.live` adds the scripts that
  [live components](/docs/ui/live-components) and live links need.

```gleam
import lustre/attribute
import lustre/element/html

page.new("Orders")
|> page.lang("en-GB")
|> page.head([
  html.meta([
    attribute.name("description"),
    attribute.content("Every order, newest first."),
  ]),
  html.link([attribute.rel("icon"), attribute.href("/favicon.svg")]),
])
```

## Choosing the theme

A page offers a set of [themes](/docs/ui/themes). With nothing else said,
the browser picks between them using the visitor's system setting for light
or dark.

`page.theme` overrides that for one response, by putting a `data-theme`
attribute on the `<html>` element. The usual source is a cookie, so that a
visitor's choice is remembered:

```gleam
import howdy/cookie

fn orders(ctx: Context) {
  use theme <- cookie.string_or(ctx, "theme", default: "system")

  page.new("Orders")
  |> page.theme(theme)
  |> page.body([ui.h1("Orders")])
  |> page.respond(ctx)
}
```

A name the page does not offer is ignored, and the attribute is left off.
That is what makes `"system"` a good default: it is not a theme, so a
visitor who has never chosen falls through to their system setting. It also
means a cookie someone has tampered with cannot put a strange value into
your page.

Because the theme is part of the HTML the server sends, the first paint is
already in the right colours. Pages that choose the theme with JavaScript
after loading flash the wrong one first; these do not.

`ui.theme_toggle` is the button that sets the cookie. See
[Themes](/docs/ui/themes).

## Where the styles come from

Every component's CSS is written in Gleam, and a page needs that CSS to look
right. By default the page embeds it: the `<head>` gets a `<style>` element
with the theme's variables, a few base styles, and the rules for the
components used so far.

Embedding needs no extra route or file, which makes it the right choice
while you are building. Its cost is that the CSS travels with every page and
cannot be cached on its own. For a published site, write it to a file once
and link that from every page:

```gleam
page.new("Orders")
|> page.stylesheet(at: "/assets/ui.css")
```

[Publishing](/docs/ui/publishing) covers both ways of serving that file.

## A shared layout

Most sites want the same header, footer and head elements on every page.
There is nothing special to learn for that. Write a function that takes the
part that varies and returns a page:

```gleam
import howdy/ui/page.{type Page}
import lustre/element.{type Element}

pub fn layout(title: String, theme: String, content: List(Element(msg))) -> Page(msg) {
  page.new(title <> " · My shop")
  |> page.theme(theme)
  |> page.stylesheet(at: "/assets/ui.css")
  |> page.body([
    site_header(),
    ui.container([], content),
    site_footer(),
  ])
}
```

```gleam
fn orders(ctx: Context) {
  use theme <- cookie.string_or(ctx, "theme", default: "system")
  layout("Orders", theme, [ui.h1("Orders")])
  |> page.respond(ctx)
}
```

A handler can still pipe the result through `page.head` or `page.live` to
add what only that page needs.

## Without a response

`page.respond` is the usual ending. Two others give you the document
without sending it:

- `page.to_string` returns the HTML as a string, doctype included. Use it
  to set a different status, as for a `404` page, or to write pages to disk.
- `page.render` returns the document as a Lustre element.

```gleam
fn not_found(ctx: Context) {
  let html =
    page.new("Not found")
    |> page.body([ui.h1("There is nothing here")])
    |> page.to_string

  controller.html(ctx, html)
  |> controller.with_status(404)
}
```

## Testing pages

A page is an ordinary response, so send a request and look at the HTML:

```gleam
import gleam/string
import howdy/testing

pub fn orders_page_test() {
  let res = testing.get("/orders") |> testing.send(app())

  assert res.status == 200
  assert string.contains(testing.text(res), "<title>Orders</title>")
}

pub fn theme_cookie_is_applied_test() {
  let res =
    testing.get("/orders")
    |> testing.cookie("theme", "dark")
    |> testing.send(app())

  assert string.contains(testing.text(res), "<html data-theme=\"dark\"")
}
```

Assert on the text a visitor would see, not on class names. Class names are
made from the content of each style, so they change whenever a style does.
